{
  "id": "home/Base_Reveal",
  "name": "A figure that plays once",
  "plugin": "biom-doc",
  "variables": {},
  "sections": [
    {
      "name": "how-to-use-this",
      "html": "<!-- guest/sections/default.html — what a section with no `data:` resolves to.\n\n     ONE CENTRED SLOT NAMED `body`, at a reading measure.\n\n     THIS IS A FILE AND NOT A BRANCH IN THE RUNTIME, and that is load-bearing\n     rather than tidy. A default that lived in the runtime would be the one\n     section shape nothing else could reach: it would need no HTML file, no\n     `data:` key and no slot declaration, and every other section would be the\n     exception to it. \"The document\" would be privileged all over again —\n     exactly what the deleted render layer kept collapsing into, where one\n     render owned the whole sheet and prose was one column at one measure\n     because there was nowhere else for it to be.\n\n     As a file it is the same mechanism every other section uses. It is read by\n     the server, sent inline in the `page.read` answer like any section's markup,\n     and drawn by the same code path. It is legible, it can be copied as the\n     starting point for a real section, and it can be replaced. A page that is\n     mostly defaults is an ordinary document. The runtime still marks one\n     `data-g-default` so the editor can tell a default section from an authored\n     one; no checker rule reads it.\n\n     THE MEASURE LIVES HERE, ON THE SECTION, RATHER THAN ON THE PAGE. That is\n     what makes a full-bleed section cost nothing: it simply does not carry this\n     file's `max-width`, and there is no sheet-level width for it to fight.\n\n     AND THE MEASURE IS FOR PROSE. When the one slot holds a grid — the\n     document's own table, which the doc plugin makes out of every markdown\n     table it finds in a paragraph and puts in a section of its own with no\n     file of its own, which is this one — the section widens to the figure\n     frame's width and the grid takes it, scrolling sideways inside its own box\n     only past that. The runtime says what a slot holds on the slot, as\n     `data-g-kind`, so this file can size by it without knowing what any\n     plugin draws; a section with a file of its own says its own width. -->\n\n<!-- No colour is set here, deliberately. Every colour in this product resolves\n     to a token the palette rewrites at runtime, and a section that reached for\n     one would be the section that stopped theming. Type and colour are inherited\n     from what the shim declares on `:root` when the theme arrives. -->\n<style>\n  /* `:scope` is this section's own element. The runtime wraps everything in this\n     file in `@scope (#sec-<name>)`, so these rules cannot reach the section\n     above or the section below however ordinary the selectors look. */\n  :scope {\n    display: block;\n  }\n\n  .measure {\n    /* The workspace's own reading measure when its `markdown.yaml` names one,\n       and 34rem when it does not. The scale OFFERS the value on the page root\n       and imposes it nowhere: this file is the section that wants it, which is\n       what keeps a full-bleed section free of it. */\n    max-width: var(--md-measure, 34rem);\n    margin-inline: auto;\n    padding-block: 1.5rem;\n    padding-inline: 1.25rem;\n  }\n\n  /* A GRID IS NOT PROSE. The measure opens to the figure frame's width — the\n     same 64rem the doc plugin's `.wrap` gives a figure — and the slot inside\n     it lets go of the reading measure the frame holds every slot to. An\n     unlayered rule here beats the frame's layered one by existing, which is\n     the frame's own bargain. */\n  .measure:has(> [data-g-kind=\"grid\"]) { max-width: 64rem; }\n  [data-g-kind=\"grid\"] { max-inline-size: none; }\n</style>\n\n<div class=\"measure\">\n  <div data-g-part=\"body\"></div>\n</div>\n",
      "fallback": true,
      "parts": {
        "body": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "md": "Copy `index.html` beside a page's `content.yaml` — `run.html` is the obvious name — and\npaste the section below into that page's `contents` with `data:` changed to match. Or copy\nthis whole directory into `pages/<parent>/children/<name>/` and it draws as it stands.\n\n**The motion is in this file and the timing is not.** `reveal` adds one class the first\ntime the section comes into view and then disconnects its observer; what happens under that\nclass is the section's own `<style>`, which is where a drawing's motion belongs.\n\n**Two things go wrong without it, and both are invisible until somebody notices the page is\ntired.** An observer that never disconnects keeps firing for as long as the page is open and\noutlives the section when it is redrawn. And an animation that starts at load plays the\nwhole choreography below the fold, so a reader who scrolls down arrives after it finished.\n\n**`--motion` is the frame's reduced-motion switch** and it is already on the page — multiply\na duration or a delay by it and a reader who asked for less motion gets none. The one thing\na multiplier cannot do is put the ink back, which is what the `prefers-reduced-motion` block\nat the foot of the file is for: stillness gets the FINISHED figure, not an empty one.\n",
          "vars": {}
        }
      },
      "vars": {},
      "source": {
        "name": "how-to-use-this",
        "parts": {
          "body": "Copy `index.html` beside a page's `content.yaml` — `run.html` is the obvious name — and\npaste the section below into that page's `contents` with `data:` changed to match. Or copy\nthis whole directory into `pages/<parent>/children/<name>/` and it draws as it stands.\n\n**The motion is in this file and the timing is not.** `reveal` adds one class the first\ntime the section comes into view and then disconnects its observer; what happens under that\nclass is the section's own `<style>`, which is where a drawing's motion belongs.\n\n**Two things go wrong without it, and both are invisible until somebody notices the page is\ntired.** An observer that never disconnects keeps firing for as long as the page is open and\noutlives the section when it is redrawn. And an animation that starts at load plays the\nwhole choreography below the fold, so a reader who scrolls down arrives after it finished.\n\n**`--motion` is the frame's reduced-motion switch** and it is already on the page — multiply\na duration or a delay by it and a reader who asked for less motion gets none. The one thing\na multiplier cannot do is put the ink back, which is what the `prefers-reduced-motion` block\nat the foot of the file is for: stillness gets the FINISHED figure, not an empty one.\n"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "stages",
      "html": "<!-- base/reveal/index.html — A FIGURE THAT ARRIVES, ONCE, AND THEN RESTS.\n\n     WHAT IT DRAWS. A run of stages: a line that draws across to where the work\n     has actually got to, a mark per stage lighting one after another as it\n     passes, the stage it reached ringed in the one hot colour, and a count\n     beside it. All of it HTML and CSS on the palette's tokens — no library, no\n     picture, nothing fetched — because that is what a figure is in this format.\n\n     WHERE THE WORDS LIVE. In `content.yaml`. The prose is markdown parts; the\n     FIGURE's own words are the section's `variables` — `stageName`, one entry\n     per mark, and `reached`, which names the one the run has got to. So editing\n     the document edits the drawing, and a sixth stage is a sixth name.\n\n     WHAT TO CHANGE FIRST: `stageName` and `reached`, then the lede beside them.\n     A figure's values are variables rather than a list SLOT, and that is the\n     rule rather than this file's preference: a slot holds markdown, so reading a\n     figure back out of a slot is parsing, and a list slot a reader can DRAG\n     would put mark 3 under stage 2 with nothing on screen to say so. Variables\n     are edited on the page's own config screen, which the note says on the page\n     rather than leaving somebody to find out. `base/list/` is the other half of\n     that answer, for the things a reader really should be able to add to.\n\n     THE OBSERVER IS A PLUGIN AND NOT FIVE LINES IN THIS FILE, and that is the\n     rule rather than a convenience: anything with even a small chance of being\n     wanted on another page is a plugin from the start. Those five lines were\n     written into 89 section files in one workspace, and the two ways they go\n     wrong — an observer that never disconnects, and an animation that starts at\n     load — are both invisible until somebody notices the page is tired.\n\n     `reveal` ADDS A CLASS AND NOTHING ELSE. `is-seen` lands on this section the\n     first time it comes into view, and the observer disconnects. Everything\n     under that class is below, which is where a drawing's motion belongs.\n\n     `--motion` IS THE FRAME'S SWITCH and is already on the page: the document\n     declares it as 1, and 0 under `prefers-reduced-motion`. A duration\n     multiplied by it is nothing when the reader has asked for less — and the\n     `@media` block at the foot is the half a multiplier cannot do, which is\n     putting the ink and the line back so stillness is the FINISHED figure.\n\n     THE STAGGER IS A CUSTOM PROPERTY. The script sets `--i` to a mark's own\n     index — the one thing a script may write onto an element's style — and the\n     stylesheet decides what the delay does with it, so the timing is readable in\n     one place rather than spread through the JavaScript.\n\n     THE FIGURE IS LEGIBLE AT EVERY FRAME. The rail, the names and the count are\n     there before anything moves; what arrives is the line and the marks. A\n     reveal that starts from nothing is a section that is blank while it plays. -->\n<style>\n  :scope { display: block; container-type: inline-size; padding-block: 2.5rem; }\n\n  .wrap {\n    display: grid;\n    gap: 1.25rem;\n    max-inline-size: 60rem;\n    margin-inline: auto;\n    padding-inline: 1.25rem;\n    font-family: var(--sheet-face);\n    color: var(--ink);\n    line-height: 1.55;\n  }\n  .wrap > * > :first-child { margin-block-start: 0; }\n  .wrap > * > :last-child { margin-block-end: 0; }\n\n  .figure {\n    display: grid;\n    gap: 1.1rem;\n    padding: 1rem 1.15rem 1.15rem;\n    border: 1px solid var(--rule);\n    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--stock-hi) 65%, transparent);\n  }\n\n  /* THE COUNT, in the gauge role with tabular figures, because a number that\n     shifts as it counts is a number nobody can read twice. */\n  .gauge { display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: .3rem; font-family: var(--gauge-face); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }\n  .gauge .now { font-size: 1.6rem; line-height: 1; color: var(--cyan-t); }\n  .gauge .of { font-size: 1.05rem; color: var(--ink-3); }\n\n  /* THE TRACK. The rail runs behind the marks at the height of their centres, so\n     the line and the stages on it are one object rather than two rows. */\n  .track { position: relative; }\n  .rail {\n    position: absolute;\n    inset-inline: 0;\n    inset-block-start: .45rem;\n    block-size: 1px;\n    background: var(--rule);\n  }\n  /* THE LINE THAT DRAWS ACROSS. It is as long as the run has got — a percentage\n     the script computes — and it grows into that length from the left. */\n  .fill {\n    display: block;\n    block-size: 100%;\n    inline-size: var(--at, 0%);\n    background: var(--cyan);\n    transform-origin: left center;\n    transform: scaleX(0);\n    transition: transform calc(760ms * var(--motion, 1)) cubic-bezier(.4, 0, .2, 1);\n  }\n\n  /* A ROW OF MARKS, sharing the track equally. Nothing here names how many. */\n  .marks { position: relative; display: flex; }\n  .stage { flex: 1 1 0; min-inline-size: 0; display: grid; justify-items: center; gap: .5rem; }\n  .dot {\n    inline-size: .9rem;\n    block-size: .9rem;\n    border: 1px solid var(--rule);\n    border-radius: 50%;\n    background: var(--stock);\n    transform: scale(.3);\n    opacity: 0;\n    transition: transform calc(300ms * var(--motion, 1)) cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1),\n                opacity calc(240ms * var(--motion, 1)) ease;\n    transition-delay: calc((260ms + var(--i, 0) * 150ms) * var(--motion, 1));\n  }\n  /* ONE HOT COLOUR, AND THE STAGE THE RUN IS AT WEARS IT LOUDEST. Everything\n     behind it is filled and everything ahead of it is an outline — a figure\n     where everything is lit has lit nothing. */\n  .stage.is-done .dot { background: var(--cyan); border-color: var(--cyan); }\n  .stage.is-here .dot { box-shadow: 0 0 0 .22rem color-mix(in srgb, var(--cyan) 22%, transparent); }\n  .name { font-size: .72rem; line-height: 1.25; color: var(--ink-3); text-align: center; }\n  .stage.is-here .name { color: var(--ink); }\n\n  /* AND THE CLASS THE `reveal` PLUGIN ADDS. It is already on the section by the\n     time a stage added later is drawn, so a mark that arrives after the reveal\n     has fired arrives finished rather than invisible. */\n  :scope.is-seen .fill { transform: none; }\n  :scope.is-seen .dot { transform: none; opacity: 1; }\n\n  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n    .fill { transform: none; }\n    .dot { transform: none; opacity: 1; }\n  }\n\n  .note { color: var(--ink-3); font-size: .88rem; }\n\n  /* THE FAULT IS A SLOT LIKE EVERY OTHER WORD HERE. A blank rectangle where a\n     drawing was is indistinguishable from a page that is still loading. */\n  .fault { display: none; color: var(--ink-3); font-size: .9rem; }\n  :scope.is-fault .fault { display: block; }\n  :scope.is-fault .figure { display: none; }\n\n  /* NARROW: the names give up first, because a phone has room for the run or\n     for the words under it and not both — and the last thing to go is the name\n     of the stage the run is actually at, which is the one fact the figure is\n     for. It has to come last, because at equal specificity the later rule\n     wins. */\n  @container (width <= 34rem) {\n    .name { font-size: .64rem; }\n  }\n  @container (width <= 26rem) {\n    .stage:not(.is-here) .name { display: none; }\n    .stage.is-here .name { font-size: .72rem; }\n  }\n</style>\n\n<div class=\"wrap\">\n  <div data-g-part=\"lede\"></div>\n  <div class=\"figure\">\n    <div class=\"gauge\"></div>\n    <div class=\"track\">\n      <div class=\"rail\"><span class=\"fill\"></span></div>\n      <div class=\"marks\"></div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"fault\" data-g-part=\"fault\"></div>\n  <!-- ONE CLASS, ONCE, ON THIS SECTION. `data-g-on` would narrow it to one\n       element inside the section; with nothing named it is the section itself. -->\n  <span data-g-plugin=\"biom-reveal\"></span>\n  <div class=\"note\" data-g-part=\"note\"></div>\n</div>\n<script>\n  /* THE DRAWING, AND NOTHING ELSE. Every word in it comes out of `ctx.vars` —\n     the page's variables and the section's, merged with the nearest last. */\n\n  /** A variable that is a list, a lone scalar, or absent — all three arrive here\n   *  and leave as an array of strings. A one-item list is written as a scalar by\n   *  most people writing YAML. */\n  function list(value) {\n    if (Array.isArray(value)) return value.map(function (v) { return String(v).trim(); });\n    if (value === undefined || value === null || value === \"\") return [];\n    return [String(value).trim()];\n  }\n\n  /** One element, with its class, appended where it belongs. */\n  function add(parent, tag, cls) {\n    const el = document.createElement(tag);\n    if (cls) el.setAttribute(\"class\", cls);\n    parent.appendChild(el);\n    return el;\n  }\n\n  const vars = ctx.vars || {};\n  const names = list(vars.stageName).filter(Boolean);\n  const reached = String(vars.reached === undefined || vars.reached === null ? \"\" : vars.reached).trim();\n\n  /* THE STAGE REACHED IS NAMED, NOT NUMBERED. An index typed into a second\n     variable is a number that is wrong the moment a stage is added in front of\n     it, and nothing on screen would say so. A name that matches nothing leaves\n     the run at the start, which is the honest drawing of a typo. */\n  const here = names.indexOf(reached);\n\n  if (names.length === 0) {\n    section.classList.add(\"is-fault\");\n  } else {\n    const marks = section.querySelector(\".marks\");\n    const gauge = section.querySelector(\".gauge\");\n    const fill = section.querySelector(\".fill\");\n\n    add(gauge, \"span\", \"now\").textContent = String(here + 1);\n    add(gauge, \"span\", \"of\").textContent = \"/ \" + names.length;\n\n    /* HOW FAR THE LINE GOES is the middle of the mark it stops at, because every\n       stage sits at the centre of its own share of the track. */\n    const at = names.length > 1 ? ((here + 0.5) / names.length) * 100 : 100;\n    fill.style.setProperty(\"--at\", Math.max(0, at) + \"%\");\n\n    /* THE DRAWING SAYS WHAT IT IS to a reader who is not looking at it, in the\n       document's own words. */\n    marks.setAttribute(\"role\", \"img\");\n    marks.setAttribute(\"aria-label\", names.join(\", \"));\n\n    names.forEach(function (name, i) {\n      const stage = add(marks, \"div\", \"stage\" + (i <= here ? \" is-done\" : \"\") + (i === here ? \" is-here\" : \"\"));\n      /* THE ONE THING A SCRIPT MAY WRITE ONTO AN ELEMENT'S STYLE IS A CUSTOM\n         PROPERTY. The stylesheet decides what the stagger does with it. */\n      const dot = add(stage, \"span\", \"dot\");\n      dot.style.setProperty(\"--i\", String(i));\n      add(stage, \"span\", \"name\").textContent = name;\n    });\n  }\n</script>\n",
      "fallback": false,
      "parts": {
        "lede": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "md": "## Where the run has got to\n\nFive stages, drawn from five names. The line grows to the one `reached` names, the marks\nlight one after another as it passes them, and the stage it stops at is the only thing on\nthe drawing wearing the hot colour — a figure where everything is lit has lit nothing.\n",
          "vars": {
            "stageName": [
              "Sown",
              "Pricked out",
              "Potted on",
              "Hardened off",
              "Planted out"
            ],
            "reached": "Potted on"
          }
        },
        "note": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "md": "A figure's values are `variables` rather than a list slot, because a slot holds markdown and\nreading a figure back out of prose is parsing. Variables are edited on this page's config\nscreen rather than on the page; `base/list/` is the shape for the things a reader really\nshould be able to add to from where they stand. Nothing here is real content.\n",
          "vars": {
            "stageName": [
              "Sown",
              "Pricked out",
              "Potted on",
              "Hardened off",
              "Planted out"
            ],
            "reached": "Potted on"
          }
        },
        "fault": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "md": "_This figure has no stages yet. Add `stageName` to the section's `variables` — one name per\nmark — and name the one the run has got to in `reached`._\n",
          "vars": {
            "stageName": [
              "Sown",
              "Pricked out",
              "Potted on",
              "Hardened off",
              "Planted out"
            ],
            "reached": "Potted on"
          }
        }
      },
      "vars": {
        "stageName": [
          "Sown",
          "Pricked out",
          "Potted on",
          "Hardened off",
          "Planted out"
        ],
        "reached": "Potted on"
      },
      "source": {
        "name": "stages",
        "data": "index.html",
        "parts": {
          "lede": "## Where the run has got to\n\nFive stages, drawn from five names. The line grows to the one `reached` names, the marks\nlight one after another as it passes them, and the stage it stops at is the only thing on\nthe drawing wearing the hot colour — a figure where everything is lit has lit nothing.\n",
          "note": "A figure's values are `variables` rather than a list slot, because a slot holds markdown and\nreading a figure back out of prose is parsing. Variables are edited on this page's config\nscreen rather than on the page; `base/list/` is the shape for the things a reader really\nshould be able to add to from where they stand. Nothing here is real content.\n",
          "fault": "_This figure has no stages yet. Add `stageName` to the section's `variables` — one name per\nmark — and name the one the run has got to in `reached`._\n"
        },
        "variables": {
          "stageName": [
            "Sown",
            "Pricked out",
            "Potted on",
            "Hardened off",
            "Planted out"
          ],
          "reached": "Potted on"
        }
      }
